================================================================================
WHAT IS WRONG

generateParse chooses between AND and OR for a rule's emitted parse condition.
A rule whose list holds ATTRIBUTES is a sequence -- every term must match, so
AND is right. A rule whose list holds MEMBERS is an alternation, because in a
kant rule members are rule options -- so OR is right. KantParserHow in
DesignDocs states the design.

The measurement says the discriminant never picks OR. StatemenT carries zero
attributes and five members, which is the textbook alternation, and it is
emitted as

    if SemI() AND BlocK() AND WardeD() AND Iterate() AND Xpress();

Across a whole walk of the grammar, 36 bodies carry AND and NOT ONE carries OR.

WHY IT MATTERS, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE CRASH

This is a CORRECTNESS threat to the final POP and not a crash candidate. An
alternation emitted as a conjunction produces a WRONG parse, not an unbounded
one -- it demands that all five options match where any one should do. So a
future parser(Start) that runs to completion could still parse incorrectly,
and the verifier's printed ANSWER would be the only thing standing between a
wrong parse and a green run.

HOW IT WAS FIXED, IN TWO RULINGS ON TWO DAYS

The original NEXT block guessed BARE-NAME RESOLUTION -- that generateParse's
bare hasAttributeS was answering about the wrong node. THAT IS DEAD. Read the
same spelling against an explicit argument.hasAttributeS on the same node and
the two agree; the node was right all along. What it was ASKING was wrong,
which is the other half of the kill condition this file wrote for itself.

Tony's diagnosis replaced it: setParse hangs builtinParsE and builtinActoR on
every rule it touches, both noPrint, so hasAttributeS -- which answers "is this
node marked up" -- reads TRUE for every walked rule and the OR branch is
unreachable.

REMEDY PART ONE, 2026-08-26. hasTraits, a GroupBody flag raised only by an
attribute that is NOT noPrint-class, registered at four sites and written by
addAttribute and updateContentFlags, with generateParse's gate swapped to it.
It read correctly on a pristine node and WRONGLY after setParse, and the 2x2
showed why in one table: the gate spelling changed nothing in either column.
setParse adds each attribute FIRST and marks it noPrint SECOND, so addAttribute
could not see that the decoration was decoration at the one instant it looked.

REMEDY PART TWO, 2026-08-27. setParse calls field.updateContentFlags() once,
after both attributes are fully formed -- a rebuild AT EXIT, which works with
the data flow instead of fighting it. The co-writer was already correct, so the
dual-writer ruling of the day before had pre-built this half without knowing it.

⚠ AND IT COST A THIRD EDIT NOBODY PLANNED, which is the part worth carrying.
updateContentFlags read `if listLength` through an unguarded groupList and had
done so forever. It was LATENT because moveTo was its only caller; the moment
setParse became the second one and started handing it leaves, it died at
EXC_BAD_ACCESS on the first listless term and took two fleet rows with it. A
helper with no live caller has never had to survive anything. See designDocs
ProblemRecords firstCallerNullList.

